{"name":"Dar es Salaam","slug":"dar-es-salaam","region":"Africa","lat":-6.79,"lon":39.21,"seasons":{"DJF":{"season_name":"Dec 2026–Feb 2027","years":[1951,1952,1953,1954,1955,1956,1957,1958,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1966,1967,1968,1969,1970,1971,1972,1973,1974,1975,1976,1977,1978,1979,1980,1981,1982,1983,1984,1985,1986,1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1993,1994,1995,1996,1997,1998,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025],"roni_lead":[-0.38,0.98,0.04,0.84,-0.31,-1.31,-0.14,1.16,0.24,0.04,0.08,-0.16,-0.3,1.06,-0.64,1.86,-0.03,-0.08,0.56,0.67,-0.57,-0.4,1.82,-1.63,-0.45,-0.95,1.04,0.89,-0.08,0.52,-0.06,-0.17,2.03,-0.9,-0.61,-0.18,1.0,1.2,-1.71,-0.23,0.27,1.2,0.1,0.46,0.97,-0.93,-0.25,2.22,-1.33,-1.06,-0.55,-0.18,1.18,0.17,0.7,-0.18,0.73,-1.33,-0.38,0.86,-1.61,-0.91,0.21,-0.22,0.35,2.03,-0.96,-0.82,0.62,0.15,-1.37,-1.08,-1.1,1.3,-0.76],"paragraph":"Dec–Feb in Dar es Salaam has warmed by about 0.25 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 1.7 °C in all, with the coldest days warming fastest, so cold extremes are becoming rarer. There is a suggestive but uncertain El Niño signal on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding Sep–Nov, Dec–Feb tends to run warmer. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about 60% of a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 9 years of the long-term trend. El Niño moves the hottest days most: this season they are expected to shift by about 0.5 °C, against 0 °C for the coldest days. Dec–Feb rainfall has fallen by about 5% per decade. El Niño has a clear effect toward a wetter season, and this year's shift is substantial. Relative humidity has fallen by about 0.4 points per decade, roughly 3 points since 1951; because warmer air holds more moisture, this does not necessarily mean the air is drier. El Niño has a strong, well-established effect toward a more humid Dec–Feb, and this year's shift is substantial. For Dec 2026–Feb 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is likely (about 90% chance), about 0.7 °C above the 1991–2020 average, mostly because of the long-term warming trend rather than El Niño; a wetter-than-normal season is more likely than not (about 65%), and a more humid season than normal is likely (about 70% chance). The El Niño expected for Sep–Nov 2026 is stronger than any in the record used here, so the outlook extends the historical relationship beyond what has been observed. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":27.4285,"trend_per_decade":0.2277,"trend_p":0.0,"trend_total_since_1951":1.7077,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.0829,"enso_p":0.0863,"confidence":"Suggestive","survives_fdr":false,"fraction_explained":0.0403,"intrinsic_sd":0.3791,"effect_this_season":0.2097,"effect_ratio":0.5533,"size":"Substantial","size_typical_el_nino":"Noticeable","once_every_n_years_without_el_nino":3,"expected_anomaly_2027":0.7088,"forecast_mean":28.1373,"forecast_sd":0.4111,"p_below":0.0226,"p_near":0.0783,"p_above":0.8991,"forecast_words":"Likely (90%): a warmer-than-normal season","within_record":false,"trend_part_2027":0.4991,"roni_upper_within_record":false,"years_of_warming_equivalent":9.2119,"percentiles":{"p05":{"trend_per_decade":0.2384,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":-0.0078,"enso_p":0.8939,"confidence":"No identifiable effect","shift_this_season":-0.0197,"lines_cross":false},"p50":{"trend_per_decade":0.2396,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.0855,"enso_p":0.0486,"confidence":"Clear","shift_this_season":0.2163,"lines_cross":false},"p95":{"trend_per_decade":0.1871,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.194,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":0.4908,"lines_cross":false},"tails":{"enso_difference":0.2018,"enso_difference_p":0.0002,"trend_difference":-0.0513,"trend_difference_p":0.0696}},"values":[25.909,25.793,25.61,25.999,26